  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nEarly this morning, the UP sits on the northern periphery of a \nmidlevel ridge spanning most of the central CONUS, which is \ndirecting a plume of moisture from the Plains northward over Lake \nSuperior and Ontario. Closer to the surface, low pressure continues \nto weaken over the UP, though another surface low is deepening over \nnortheastern CO, while surface high pressure remains sprawled out \nover central Canada. This has helped to push a frontal boundary \nsouthward into the area; it can currently be seen stalled out from \naround the Bayfield peninsula to the Keweenaw and then northeast \nacross the lake. This will be the focus for our next rain chances \nlater today, but so far, dry conditions persist, with temperatures \nso far only in the upper 50s and lower 60s still looking to bottom \nout in the mid the upper 50s for most. \n\nAs we head into the daytime hours, the Colorado low begins to move \nnortheastward towards the area, leading to an increasing pressure \ngradient across Superior and the northern UP. Northeast winds pick \nup, with gusts up to 20-25mph closer to Superior and possibly up to \n30mph over the Keweenaw. After daybreak, chances for rain showers \nincrease over the lake and the far northern UP, closer in proximity \nto the boundary, as the plume of moisture works southward. \nMeanwhile, expect high temperatures to range largely in the lower to \nmid 60s behind the boundary (that is, from around Ironwood to \nMarquette) while northeast winds limit areas nearer Superior and in \nthe Keweenaw to the 50s. To the south and east, ahead of the front, \ntemperatures peak in the lower to mid 70s, though the south-central \nUP may climb a bit further into the mid/upper 70s. Rain totals \nremain quite light before the low moves through the area this \nevening into tonight, touching off more widespread rain showers \nacross the UP. Subsequent NBM runs have steadily inched 24-hour QPF \ntotals up, with a widespread 0.25-0.50in expected across the UP by \nTuesday morning. However, there is around a 50-70% chance for totals \nin excess of a half inch (highest north), given the potential for \nmultiple rounds of rainfall as well as some embedded thunderstorms. \nRain showers quickly wrap up Tuesday morning, while cooler air \nworking in brings a return to more seasonal temperatures. Expect \ntemperatures to climb from morning lows in the 40s and lower 50s to \nhighs in the 50s to mid 60s.\n\nHigh pressure moves in Wednesday, bringing a return to dry weather \nwhile temperatures trend more seasonal while a midlevel low \n(originating from the deepening trough currently over the Pac NW) \nparks over the Rockies. Thursday onwards, the general setup among \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance is for that low to slowly move \nout over the Plains while a deepening trough drops out of Alberta. \nCompared to 24 hours ago, things are looking a little more \nprogressive, with guidance showing a phasing of these two systems \nsomewhere near the Great Lakes Thursday, resulting in a rapidly \ndeepening surface low over the Lower Great Lakes sometime later \nThursday into Friday. As hinted at, there remains a fair amount of \nuncertainty in the particulars on strength, timing, and track. \nStill, we do face the potential for more rounds of wet weather \ntowards the end of the work week, as well as a shot of colder air \ninto the weekend, though confidence in the finer points is low.",
